6. ANDROID SCREEN SHARING

Implement real Android screen capture using MediaProjection.

Official Android documentation:
https://developer.android.com/media/grow/media-projection

6.1 Screen sharing workflow

When the user taps "Share Screen":

1. Check Android permissions and service requirements.
2. Launch the official MediaProjection consent flow.
3. Wait for the user to grant permission.
4. Start the required foreground service.
5. Initialize MediaProjection.
6. Create a virtual display.
7. Capture frames using ImageReader or an equivalent supported approach.
8. Process frames.
9. Send frames to Gemini Live API.
10. Continue until the user stops sharing or Android revokes the projection.

Do not bypass the Android system permission dialog.

Do not capture the screen before permission is granted.

6.2 Android compatibility

Respect modern Android MediaProjection restrictions.

In particular:

- Follow Android 14+ consent and token lifecycle requirements.
- Do not reuse a MediaProjection grant improperly.
- Register and handle MediaProjection callbacks.
- Handle user-initiated stopping.
- Handle screen lock and application termination.
- Handle service destruction.
- Handle projection revocation.
- Use the correct foreground service type and permissions for the target Android version.

Keep compatibility logic version-aware.

6.3 Frame processing

Do not send unprocessed full-resolution screenshots continuously.

Implement a FrameProcessor.

Responsibilities:

- Capture frames.
- Resize images.
- Maintain aspect ratio.
- Compress to JPEG.
- Avoid memory leaks.
- Release ImageReader images promptly.
- Avoid blocking the main thread.
- Discard obsolete frames.
- Limit unnecessary duplicate frames.

Initial recommended configuration:

Setting| Initial value
Frame rate| 1 FPS
Maximum image dimension| 1280 px
Image format| JPEG
JPEG quality| 70
Frame queue| Maximum 1 pending frame
Processing| Background dispatcher

These values must be configurable internally.

Important: Gemini Live video processing has model/API-specific limitations, including documented 1 FPS video processing. Do not promise smooth, frame-by-frame video understanding.

If the screen remains unchanged, avoid sending redundant frames where possible.

If the screen changes, send the latest available frame.

Never allow a growing frame queue.

The AI should prioritize the latest screen state.

6.4 Screen capture lifecycle

Implement:

- startCapture()
- stopCapture()
- pauseCapture()
- resumeCapture()
- releaseCapture()

The stop operation must:

- Stop the frame loop.
- Release VirtualDisplay.
- Close ImageReader.
- Stop MediaProjection.
- Release related resources.
- Update application state.

Ensure no capture process remains running after the user stops sharing.

